-- allium: 3
-- epics.allium
--
-- Scope: Epic lifecycle and MCP epic tools
-- Excludes: Task lifecycle (see tasks.allium), domain model (see core.allium)

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Managed-feed configuration surface. These are app-level settings (not fields
-- on any epic): two feed scripts, each with an optional poll interval. They
-- drive ProvisionManagedEpics, which materialises the corresponding managed
-- epic tree. All four default to "unset": a null command means that subtree is
-- not provisioned at all (provisioning is opt-in). When a command is set but
-- its interval is null, the epic falls back to core/config.default_feed_interval,
-- exactly as for hand-made feed epics.
--
-- NOTE — these four settings are editable ONLY over MCP, via
-- set_managed_feed_config (read back with get_managed_feed_config). There is no
-- in-app TUI editor. On save, that surface persists the settings and re-fires
-- ProvisionManagedEpics, so a newly-enabled feed provisions its epic tree
-- without a restart.
config {
    reviews_feed_command: String? = null         -- script emitting the involved-PR feed (see feeds.allium); null = reviews subtree not provisioned
    reviews_feed_interval_secs: Integer? = null   -- poll cadence for the reviews_parent root; null falls back to default_feed_interval
    cve_feed_command: String? = null              -- script emitting the security/CVE feed; null = cve epic not provisioned
    cve_feed_interval_secs: Integer? = null       -- poll cadence for the cve root; null falls back to default_feed_interval
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Editor ==

rule EditEpic {
    when: EditEpic(epic)

    -- Opens the user's editor — resolved by the shared rule stated with
    -- core/config.editor_fallback, the same resolution EditTask uses — with a
    -- structured file containing the epic's
    -- title, description, feed_command and feed_interval_secs. The file
    -- uses "--- SECTION ---" delimiters (TITLE, DESCRIPTION,
    -- FEED_COMMAND, FEED_INTERVAL_SECS).
    -- The user edits the file; on save, fields are parsed back.
    -- For title and description, an empty field retains the previous
    -- value. For feed_command and feed_interval_secs, an empty field
    -- clears the value (sets it to null) — this is how feeds are turned
    -- off from the editor.
    -- FEED_INTERVAL_SECS accepts the shared interval-literal grammar
    -- (core.allium: "How a human types an interval"), so "10m" and "600" are
    -- the same edit. It is written back as bare seconds, so a suffixed value
    -- reappears unsuffixed on the next open. The bare-integer form has always
    -- meant seconds here and still does, which is what makes suffix support
    -- purely additive for every feed epic configured before it existed.
    -- FEED_INTERVAL_SECS otherwise follows the same parse rule as EditTask's
    -- enum-ish sections: an empty section and an unparseable section are
    -- treated identically — the section yields no value, so the field
    -- is cleared. Unparseable input additionally records a parse error, and
    -- all accumulated errors are surfaced together as a status message
    -- ("Edit accepted with parse errors — ..."); the edit is still applied.
    -- Status, plan_path, sort_order and auto_dispatch are not editable
    -- via the epic editor.
    --
    -- The interval grammar and the interval FLOOR are enforced in different
    -- places, and this section is where a user meets the seam. The grammar
    -- only parses, so "10s" is a SUCCESSFUL parse — no parse error is
    -- recorded — and is then refused on save by the floor
    -- (core.allium: "Interval literals", CLAIM 2). A refusal is not the
    -- parse-error path above: the save goes through UpdateEpic as one
    -- validated call, so a refused interval rejects the WHOLE edit. No field
    -- is applied, not even an unrelated title change in the same session, and
    -- the validation error is surfaced as an error status message rather than
    -- the "Edit accepted with parse errors" one.
    --
    -- A refused edit MUST leave the board showing the stored epic. Reporting
    -- an error and then rendering the refused value is the worst of both: the
    -- user is told the save failed while looking at evidence it succeeded, and
    -- the display only self-corrects on the next DB refresh.

    ensures:
        let edited = EditorSession(epic)
        -- A sub-floor interval refuses the whole edit, so every clause below
        -- is guarded on acceptance: when the interval is refused NO field
        -- changes, and the epic the board shows is the stored one.
        let accepted = edited.feed_interval_secs = null
                       or seconds(edited.feed_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval
        if accepted and edited.title != "": epic.title = edited.title
        if accepted and edited.description != "": epic.description = edited.description
        -- feed fields clear on empty rather than retaining
        if accepted:
            if edited.feed_command != "": epic.feed_command = edited.feed_command
            else: epic.feed_command = null
        if accepted:
            if edited.feed_interval_secs != null:
                epic.feed_interval_secs = edited.feed_interval_secs
            else: epic.feed_interval_secs = null

    @guidance
        -- Unlike EditTask, the epic editor does not expose status.
        -- Epic status is derived from subtask states via the
        -- EpicStatusRecalculation invariant and is not directly
        -- editable by the user.
        -- updated_at is touched automatically by the database layer on
        -- any patch_epic call, so the rule does not assert it directly.
        -- The editor opens in a separate tmux window; tmux focus
        -- switches to that window while the TUI keeps running in
        -- its original window. On editor exit the window closes and
        -- focus returns to the TUI. At most one editor session is
        -- live at a time — a second edit while an editor is already
        -- open is a no-op with a status message.
}

-- == Epic Lifecycle ==

rule CreateEpic {
    when: CreateEpic(title, description?, sort_order?, parent?,
                     feed_command?, feed_interval_secs?)

    -- The universal feed-cadence floor (core.allium: "Interval literals",
    -- CLAIM 2). A value below it is rejected with a validation error naming
    -- the field and the minimum. Creation is bound as tightly as update:
    -- validating only the update path would leave an epic able to be BORN
    -- busy-looping.
    requires:
        if feed_interval_secs != null:
            seconds(feed_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval

    -- parent, when given, must refer to an existing epic. Checked before the
    -- insert, so a bad parent yields a not-found error and no epic at all.
    requires:
        if parent != null: core/Epic.exists(parent)

    ensures: core/Epic.created(
        title: title,
        description: description,
        status: backlog,
        sort_order: sort_order,
        parent: parent,
        feed_command: feed_command,
        feed_interval_secs: feed_interval_secs,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    -- A new backlog child changes the parent's active_sub_epics set, so the
    -- parent is recalculated immediately after the insert (e.g. a Done parent
    -- with a freshly-attached Backlog child regresses right away).
    ensures:
        if parent != null: RecalculateEpicStatus(parent)

    @guidance
        -- Invariant: the returned Epic reflects every field the caller
        -- supplied. A caller must never see an Epic reporting a field as
        -- unset when it passed a value for it.
        --
        -- CreateEpicViaMcp (below) is not a second implementation: both rules
        -- witness the one service entry point, which is where the
        -- parent-existence check and the parent recalculation live. The two
        -- rules differ only in which parameters the surface exposes.
        --
        -- Known implementation limitation (not design intent): the current
        -- insert cannot express sort_order, feed_command or
        -- feed_interval_secs, so the service writes them in a follow-up
        -- patch and re-reads. That patch's failure is an error of the whole
        -- creation. The two-write shape is not atomic — a failure between
        -- them leaves a half-populated epic — and it is not a pattern to
        -- extend: a fourth such field wants a single-insert create request
        -- (mirroring core/CreateTaskRequest) rather than another patch line.
        --
        -- When parent is provided, this creates a sub-epic nested inside
        -- the given parent. Root epics (parent = null) appear on the main board;
        -- sub-epics appear only inside their parent's epic view.
        --
        -- This grouping is bypassed when the board is in flattened mode
        -- (see board-layout.allium: FlattenedView), but only in the Running,
        -- Review and Done columns: there epic cards are hidden and every
        -- descendant task surfaces directly in its status column. Backlog is
        -- excluded from flattening — it is the column read at the epic level,
        -- so epic cards remain visible there and epic-owned tasks stay inside
        -- them.
        --
        -- Outside flattened mode an epic card is not confined to one column:
        -- it is drawn in every column where the epic's subtree has visible
        -- work (see board-layout.allium: "Epic Card Placement").
}

-- There is no batch wrap-up for an epic. Each subtask's agent wraps its
-- own work up through the MCP wrap_up tool (see pr-workflow.allium);
-- there is no board-driven merge queue that sequences rebases across an
-- epic's review tasks.

rule MoveEpicForward {
    when: MoveEpicStatus(epic, forward)

    requires: epic.status != done
    requires: epic.status != archived

    let next = next_status(epic.status)

    ensures: epic.status = next
    ensures: epic.updated_at = now
    ensures:
        if next = done:
            epic.completed_at = now
            for task in epic.subtasks where tmux_window != null:
                task.tmux_window = null

    @guidance
        -- Uses the same next() progression as tasks:
        --   backlog -> running -> review -> done.
        -- Done and archived are terminal for forward movement.
        -- When moving to done, all subtask tmux windows are killed
        -- to clean up active agent sessions.
        -- Cleanup is direct-subtasks only, unlike ArchiveEpic which
        -- recurses into sub-epic descendants.
        -- Unlike MoveTaskForward (which excludes done from forward
        -- movement entirely), MoveEpicForward allows manual forward
        -- movement directly into done. epic.completed_at = now is the same
        -- completion stamp tasks.allium's ConfirmDone applies, and the Done
        -- column reads it descending: an epic that has finished sorts by its
        -- own completion time in preference to the derived subtask key
        -- (board-layout.allium, "Done Column Ordering").
}

rule MoveEpicBackward {
    when: MoveEpicStatus(epic, backward)

    requires: epic.status != backlog
    requires: epic.status != archived

    let prev = prev_status(epic.status)

    ensures: epic.status = prev
    ensures: epic.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Uses the same prev() progression as tasks:
        --   done -> review -> running -> backlog.
        -- Backlog and archived are terminal for backward movement.
        -- Leaving done clears nothing, mirroring tasks.allium's
        -- MoveTaskBackward: epic.sort_order is manual ordering that no status
        -- write touches, and epic.completed_at records the last completion,
        -- which moving backward does not unmake.
}

rule ConfirmArchiveEpic {
    when: ConfirmArchiveEpic(epic)

    requires:
        epic.active_subtasks.count = 0
        or epic.active_subtasks.all(t => t.status = done)

    ensures: ConfirmationShown(epic, "Archive epic and all subtasks?")

    @guidance
        -- The guard looks only at active subtasks (Task). Active
        -- sub-epics do not block archiving the parent. When the user
        -- confirms, the TUI also passes the archive request through a
        -- batch skip-check that skips any individual epic whose active
        -- subtasks are not all done, reporting "Skipped N epics with
        -- non-done subtasks".
}

rule ConfirmDeleteEpic {
    when: ConfirmDeleteEpic(epic)

    -- Prompts the user with "Delete epic <title> and subtasks? [y/n]".
    -- Pressing y emits DeleteEpic(epic); any other key cancels.

    ensures: ConfirmationShown(epic, "Delete epic and subtasks?")

    @guidance
        -- This confirmation step has no other guard: deletion is
        -- allowed regardless of subtask or sub-epic state.
}

rule ArchiveEpic {
    when: ArchiveEpic(epic)

    ensures:
        for task in epic.active_subtasks:
            ArchiveTask(task)
    ensures:
        for sub_epic in epic.sub_epics:
            ArchiveEpic(sub_epic)
    ensures: epic.status = archived

    @guidance
        -- Archiving is a soft operation: the epic and its entire
        -- subtree (direct subtasks plus all nested sub-epics and their
        -- tasks) are recursively transitioned to status = archived.
        -- Nothing is deleted from the database, so the archived tree
        -- remains visible in the archive view and can be inspected
        -- after the fact. To hard-delete, use DeleteEpic instead.
        --
        -- Implementation note: archive does not exercise the DELETE
        -- code path, so it is unaffected by foreign-key references
        -- from other tables (e.g. learnings.source_task_id)
        -- to the tasks being archived.
        --
        -- If the currently active view is on the archived epic, the
        -- TUI exits back to the parent view.
        --
        -- Archiving a done epic clears nothing: epic.sort_order is not a
        -- completion field any more, and epic.completed_at survives leaving
        -- done by design (tasks.allium, ConfirmDone's guidance).
}

-- An archived epic is soft-deleted: it holds no live work and it gains none.
--
-- Intent: archived has to mean one thing everywhere, or every reader has to
-- know which kind of archived it is looking at. The board relies on this —
-- board-layout.allium draws no card for an archived epic in any column, and
-- that is only honest if the epic has nothing live behind the card it is not
-- drawing. An archived epic still owning a running task would hide that task's
-- own epic context with no way to reach it.
--
-- Three things maintain it, and all three are needed:
--   - ArchiveEpic cascades: every live subtask and every sub-epic goes archived
--     with the epic, so the invariant holds the moment archiving finishes. This
--     is why ConfirmArchiveEpic's "all subtasks must be done" guard is the only
--     guard archiving needs — the cascade does the rest.
--   - Un-archiving revives upward (ReviveEpicChainOnUnarchive above), which
--     covers the one gesture that is neither archiving nor attaching: a task or
--     sub-epic edited back out of archived in place, under an ancestor that is
--     still archived.
--   - Every path that attaches work refuses an archived target, so the
--     invariant cannot be broken afterwards: MoveTaskToEpic and
--     UpdateTaskViaMcp / CreateTaskViaMcp setting epic_id (tasks.allium,
--     mcp-task-tools.allium), and ReparentEpicFromTui / UpdateEpicViaMcp
--     setting a parent. Without the second half the first is only true until
--     the next create_task call, which is how the board came to show archived
--     epics parked in Backlog.
--
-- The guard belongs to the service layer, not to the pickers. The TUI reparent
-- and move-to-epic trees already leave archived epics out of their candidate
-- lists, but an MCP caller never sees a picker, so a filtered list is a
-- convenience and not the rule.
-- Un-archiving revives the epics above it.
--
-- Intent: ArchiveEpic cascades DOWN — an epic and its whole subtree go
-- archived together. This is the inverse, and ArchivedEpicHoldsNoLiveWork
-- needs it to survive the one gesture that is not an attachment: reviving
-- something in place. An archived task edited back to backlog (tasks.allium:
-- EditTask, whose editor is bound in the archive view too) would otherwise be
-- live work inside an epic that draws no card — invisible, with nothing on
-- screen to say why. That is the exact shape of bug the archived-card rule
-- exists to remove, arrived at from the other direction.
--
-- Refusing the revival was the alternative and is worse: it turns reviving one
-- task into a two-step job — un-archive the epic first, or move the task out —
-- and it refuses at the point the user can act on it least, from inside the
-- archive view looking at the task they want back.
--
-- Scope: the ANCESTOR CHAIN, never siblings. Reviving one task pulls back
-- exactly the epics needed to see it; the tasks and sub-epics it was archived
-- alongside stay archived. Un-archive is not undo — the cascade down archived a
-- subtree the user chose to archive, and reviving all of it from one task would
-- undo a decision they did not revisit.
--
-- A revived epic does not get its old status back: nothing records what that
-- was. It is set to backlog and then recalculated from its children like any
-- other epic (see "Epic status recalculation"), which is what settles it into
-- the column the revived task's own status earns.
rule ReviveEpicChainOnUnarchive {
    when: TaskLeftArchived(task)

    ensures:
        for epic in Epic.ancestor_chain(task.epic) where epic.status = archived:
            epic.status = backlog
    ensures: EpicStatusRecalculated(task.epic)

    @guidance
        -- Fires on any write that moves a task OUT of archived, whatever the
        -- surface: the TUI's task editor (the archive view binds the same key),
        -- and UpdateTaskViaMcp with a non-archived status. Enforced in the
        -- service rather than in either caller — src/service/tasks/crud.rs's
        -- update_task — so both surfaces get it from one place.
        --
        -- The chain is walked from the task's own epic upward and INCLUDES it.
        -- Only archived epics on that chain are touched; a live ancestor above
        -- an archived one is left exactly as it is.
        --
        -- The walk is bounded by the number of epics, so a malformed
        -- parent chain cannot spin.
}

-- The same revival, driven from an epic rather than a task: un-archiving a
-- sub-epic revives the archived epics above it, for the reason above — a live
-- sub-epic under an archived parent is a card the board will not draw.
rule ReviveParentChainOnEpicUnarchive {
    when: EpicLeftArchived(epic)

    ensures:
        for ancestor in Epic.ancestor_chain(epic) where ancestor.status = archived:
            ancestor.status = backlog

    @guidance
        -- Enforced in src/service/epics.rs's update_epic, alongside the
        -- archived-parent guard, so every caller of the epic patch seam is held
        -- to it. Descendants are NOT revived: the scope argument in
        -- ReviveEpicChainOnUnarchive applies unchanged.
}

invariant ArchivedEpicHoldsNoLiveWork {
    for epic in epics where epic.status = archived:
        epic.active_subtasks.is_empty() and epic.active_sub_epics.is_empty()
}

rule DeleteEpic {
    when: DeleteEpic(epic)

    -- Deletes epic and all linked subtasks and sub-epics (recursively)
    ensures:
        for task in epic.subtasks:
            if task.worktree != null:
                not exists task.worktree
    ensures:
        for task in epic.subtasks:
            not exists task
    ensures:
        for sub_epic in epic.sub_epics:
            DeleteEpic(sub_epic)
    ensures: not exists epic

    @guidance
        -- Deletion is performed in a single database transaction that
        -- walks parent_epic_id pointers depth-first, deleting each
        -- epic's tasks then the epic row itself. Worktrees and tmux
        -- windows are cleaned up best-effort for every subtask in that
        -- same subtree — not just the directly-owned ones — before the DB
        -- call, mirroring ArchiveEpic's recursion. The two must agree: the
        -- DB delete drops the whole subtree, so a cleanup that covered
        -- only direct children would delete a nested subtask's row while
        -- leaving its worktree, branch and tmux window on disk with
        -- nothing left referencing them (task #3897).
        -- If the currently active view is on the deleted epic, the TUI
        -- exits back to the parent view.
}

-- == MCP Epic Tools ==

rule CreateEpicViaMcp {
    when: McpCreateEpic(title, description?, sort_order?,
                        parent_epic_id?)

    -- parent_epic_id, when given, must refer to an existing epic, and that
    -- epic must not be archived: an archived epic gains no children
    -- (ArchivedEpicHoldsNoLiveWork).
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != null: core/Epic.exists(parent_epic_id)
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != null: core/Epic.find(parent_epic_id).status != archived

    ensures: core/Epic.created(
        title: title,
        description: description,
        status: backlog,
        sort_order: sort_order,
        parent_epic_id: parent_epic_id,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- feed_command and feed_interval_secs are NOT exposed by the
        -- MCP create_epic tool — even though the underlying service
        -- accepts them, the handler hardcodes them to null. Feeds are
        -- configured after creation either from the TUI (via the epic
        -- editor, EditEpic) or via UpdateEpicViaMcp.
}

rule GetEpicViaMcp {
    when: McpGetEpic(epic_id)

    requires: core/Epic.exists(epic_id)

    -- Returns the Epic record for epic_id along with a subtask summary
    -- (done_count, total_count) rolled up exactly as ListEpicsViaMcp
    -- rolls it up.
    ensures: EpicRetrieved(epic_id)

    @guidance
        -- Returns the epic's core fields (title, description,
        -- status, plan_path, sort_order, feed_command, feed_interval_secs,
        -- created_at, updated_at) plus a "Subtasks: D/T done" line
        -- where D counts subtasks with status = done and T is the total.
        -- The counts come from EpicService::get_epic_with_progress, which
        -- reuses the same rollup as list_epics_with_progress
        -- (ListEpicsViaMcp) so get_epic, list_epics and the board never
        -- disagree: for a group_by_repo epic the tasks of all descendant
        -- sub-epics are aggregated recursively; for any other epic only
        -- the direct subtasks are counted. Without the shared rollup a
        -- group_by_repo epic whose tasks all live in per-repo sub-epics
        -- reports 0/0 here while list_epics shows the real totals.
        -- An unknown epic_id returns ServiceError::NotFound, surfaced as
        -- the -32001 not-found code (distinct from -32602, which means
        -- invalid params).
}

rule ListEpicsViaMcp {
    when: McpListEpics()

    -- Returns all Epic records with per-epic subtask progress
    -- (done_count, total_count).
    ensures: EpicsListed()

    @guidance
        -- Takes no arguments. Returns every NON-ARCHIVED epic in the
        -- database along with done/total subtask counts for each;
        -- archived epics are filtered out of this list entirely (an
        -- agent cannot see them through list_epics, only through
        -- get_epic by id). The response is one line per epic carrying the
        -- epic id, the title, the progress fraction, then a plan indicator
        -- (when a plan is attached) followed by a status indicator (when
        -- the status is not backlog), and finally the description. The id
        -- is included because it is how the caller addresses the epic in
        -- follow-up calls (get_epic, update_epic, create_task).
        -- An empty database returns an informational "No epics found"
        -- message rather than an error.
}

rule UpdateEpicViaMcp {
    when: McpUpdateEpic(epic, title?, description?, status?, plan?, sort_order?,
                        feed_command?, feed_interval_secs?, group_by_repo?,
                        feed_append_only?, parent_epic_id?)

    requires: title != null or description != null or status != null
              or plan != null or sort_order != null
              or feed_command != omitted
              or feed_interval_secs != omitted or group_by_repo != null
              or feed_append_only != null
              or parent_epic_id != omitted

    -- Cannot set status to archived via MCP. The agent receives a
    -- validation error instructing them to ask the human operator to
    -- archive from the TUI.
    requires: status != archived

    -- The universal feed-cadence floor (core.allium: "Interval literals",
    -- CLAIM 2). A value below it is rejected with a validation error naming
    -- the field and the minimum. Explicit null still clears the field: that
    -- means "inherit config.default_feed_interval", which is itself at or
    -- above the floor, so clearing can never sink below it.
    requires:
        if feed_interval_secs != omitted and feed_interval_secs != null:
            seconds(feed_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval

    -- parent_epic_id validation: parent must exist and no cycle may be introduced
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != omitted and parent_epic_id != null:
            Epic.exists(parent_epic_id)
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != omitted and parent_epic_id != null:
            not Epic.ancestor_chain(parent_epic_id).contains(epic.id)
    -- An archived epic gains no children (ArchivedEpicHoldsNoLiveWork).
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != omitted and parent_epic_id != null:
            core/Epic.find(parent_epic_id).status != archived

    let prior_status = epic.status

    -- An auto-created repo-group sub-epic cannot be moved out of its
    -- grouping root. This covers BOTH reparenting (a new parent) and
    -- detaching (parent_epic_id = null): any non-omitted parent_epic_id on
    -- such an epic is rejected with a validation error, "Cannot reparent an
    -- auto-created repo-group sub-epic".
    requires:
        if parent_epic_id != omitted:
            epic.origin != repo_group

    ensures:
        if title != null: epic.title = title
    ensures:
        if description != null: epic.description = description
    ensures:
        if status != null: epic.status = status
    ensures:
        if plan != null: epic.plan_path = plan
    ensures:
        if sort_order != null: epic.sort_order = sort_order
    ensures:
        -- Entering done via the status param stamps the completion time,
        -- matching MoveEpicForward. It no longer competes with an explicit
        -- sort_order in the same call: the two write different fields now, so
        -- a caller can set both and get both. Leaving done writes nothing.
        if status != null:
            if status = done and prior_status != done:
                epic.completed_at = now
    ensures:
        -- feed_command supports three states: omitted (no change), a string
        -- (set), or explicit null (clear to NULL)
        if feed_command != omitted: epic.feed_command = feed_command
    ensures:
        -- feed_interval_secs supports three states: omitted (no change), an
        -- integer (set), or explicit null (clear to NULL)
        if feed_interval_secs != omitted: epic.feed_interval_secs = feed_interval_secs
    ensures:
        -- group_by_repo is a plain boolean set (absent = no change). Note
        -- that setting it here flips the flag ONLY — no task migration is
        -- performed. See the guidance below.
        if group_by_repo != null: epic.group_by_repo = group_by_repo
    -- An epic may be append-only OR grouped/role-routed, never both, and the
    -- restriction is permanent. Repo is a MIRRORING feed's key — its items are
    -- repo-scoped things carrying their own repo's URL. An append-only feed's
    -- items are events keyed by where in the code they fired, and the one url
    -- they carry is a configured repo root that exists only to resolve a local
    -- clone, so grouping would partition every item into a single sub-epic.
    -- Grouping by a constant. The supported way to cover several repos is one
    -- flat append-only epic per repo under a common parent. Evaluated against
    -- the POST-update values, so it catches the pair whichever flag arrives
    -- second and whether they arrive together or in separate calls; feed_role
    -- is not settable here, so it is read from the stored epic.
    -- See feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed for the full argument.
    requires:
        let appends_only = feed_append_only ?? epic.feed_append_only
        let grouped = group_by_repo ?? epic.group_by_repo
        appends_only implies (not grouped and epic.feed_role = none)

    ensures:
        -- feed_append_only is a plain boolean set (absent = no change).
        -- Unlike group_by_repo this needs no migration counterpart: the flag
        -- changes only what FUTURE cycles do, and neither direction rewrites
        -- an existing task. See feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed for what each
        -- direction costs — in particular that turning it OFF hands the next
        -- cycle licence to remove every accumulated task the emission does
        -- not name.
        if feed_append_only != null: epic.feed_append_only = feed_append_only
    ensures:
        -- parent_epic_id uses triple-state semantics: omitted = no change,
        -- integer = reparent under that epic, null = make this a root epic.
        if parent_epic_id != omitted: epic.parent_epic_id = parent_epic_id

    @guidance
        -- updated_at is refreshed automatically by the database layer
        -- on any patch_epic call, so the rule does not assert it
        -- explicitly.
        -- The auto_dispatch flag is not exposed via the MCP update
        -- tool; it is toggled only from the TUI through ToggleAutoDispatch.
        -- feed_command and feed_interval_secs use triple-state JSON
        -- semantics: absent field = no change; explicit null = clear;
        -- string/integer value = set.
        -- parent_epic_id uses the same triple-state JSON semantics.
        -- Cycle detection walks the ancestor chain of the proposed parent;
        -- if the current epic appears anywhere in that chain the update
        -- is rejected with a -32602 validation error.
        --
        -- group_by_repo is a FLAG FLIP ONLY over MCP — intentional, but
        -- consequential. The regroup/flatten task migration (RegroupEpic /
        -- FlattenEpic) fires only from the TUI toggle path
        -- (ToggleGroupByRepo, the `R` key), which deliberately inlines
        -- update-then-migrate rather than reusing this patch path. An agent
        -- that turns group_by_repo ON here leaves every existing task sitting
        -- directly on the epic, unrouted into per-repo sub-epics; turning it
        -- OFF here leaves tasks stranded inside the repo-group sub-epics
        -- rather than pulled back to the parent. The epic reports the new
        -- flag and the board renders accordingly, so the mismatch is not
        -- obviously visible. Reconciliation requires either a manual TUI
        -- toggle (which runs the migration) or, for feed epics, the next feed
        -- sync (GroupedFeedUpsert / FlatFeedReconcile in feeds.allium, which
        -- self-heal in both directions).
        --
        -- An epic with origin = repo_group cannot have its parent changed by
        -- this tool at all — neither reparented nor detached to root (see the
        -- requires above). This keeps auto-created per-repo sub-epics inside
        -- the grouping root that owns them.
        --
        -- epic.completed_at = now is the same completion stamp used by
        -- tasks.allium's ConfirmDone and this file's MoveEpicForward. Staying
        -- in done, staying outside done, or omitting status entirely all leave
        -- epic.completed_at untouched. epic.sort_order is whatever the
        -- sort_order clause above set, in every case: no status transition
        -- reads or writes it.
}

rule GetManagedFeedConfigViaMcp {
    when: McpGetManagedFeedConfig()

    -- Returns the four managed-feed config settings (reviews/CVE feed
    -- commands and their poll intervals). Unset values are reported as
    -- unset. Takes no arguments.
    ensures: ManagedFeedConfigRetrieved()
}

rule SetManagedFeedConfigViaMcp {
    when: McpSetManagedFeedConfig(reviews_command?, reviews_interval_secs?,
                                  cve_command?, cve_interval_secs?)

    -- Each field is triple-state JSON: omitted = leave the stored value
    -- unchanged; explicit null = clear to null; a value = set.
    --
    -- Both intervals are bound by the universal feed-cadence floor
    -- (core.allium: "Interval literals", CLAIM 2), rejected with a -32602
    -- validation error naming the field and the minimum. These settings are
    -- not a separate kind of cadence: they are COPIED onto the managed epics'
    -- feed_interval_secs by ProvisionManagedEpics, so a value the floor would
    -- refuse on an epic must not be reachable by writing it here instead.
    -- Nothing about a dispatch-provisioned feed makes a busy loop cheaper than
    -- a user-configured one.
    requires:
        if reviews_interval_secs != omitted and reviews_interval_secs != null:
            seconds(reviews_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval
    requires:
        if cve_interval_secs != omitted and cve_interval_secs != null:
            seconds(cve_interval_secs) >= core/config.min_feed_interval

    ensures:
        if reviews_command != omitted: config.reviews_feed_command = reviews_command
    ensures:
        if reviews_interval_secs != omitted: config.reviews_feed_interval_secs = reviews_interval_secs
    ensures:
        if cve_command != omitted: config.cve_feed_command = cve_command
    ensures:
        if cve_interval_secs != omitted: config.cve_feed_interval_secs = cve_interval_secs

    -- After persisting, re-materialise the managed epic tree from the new
    -- config.
    ensures: ProvisionManagedEpics()

    @guidance
        -- This is the only configuration path: persist the four settings, then
        -- fire ProvisionManagedEpics and refresh the board. A call with no
        -- fields is a harmless no-op that still re-provisions and reports the
        -- current config. feed commands are not newline-validated (they are
        -- shell scripts run via sh -c, matching feed_command on epics).
}

rule ReparentEpicFromTui {
    when: ReparentEpic(epic, new_parent?)

    -- parent validation: when a parent is chosen it must exist and
    -- introduce no cycle. A null new_parent makes the epic a root.
    requires:
        if new_parent != null: core/Epic.exists(new_parent)
    requires:
        if new_parent != null:
            not Epic.ancestor_chain(new_parent).contains(epic)
            and new_parent != epic

    -- An archived epic gains no children (ArchivedEpicHoldsNoLiveWork). The
    -- picker already omits archived epics, so this is the service-layer guard
    -- behind that omission and the only one an MCP caller meets.
    requires:
        if new_parent != null: core/Epic.find(new_parent).status != archived

    -- An auto-created repo-group sub-epic cannot be moved, in either
    -- direction: both a new parent and a null new_parent (detach to root)
    -- are rejected with the validation error "Cannot reparent an
    -- auto-created repo-group sub-epic".
    requires: epic.origin != repo_group

    ensures: epic.parent_epic_id = new_parent
    ensures: epic.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Triggered from the kanban board when the user presses `m` on an
        -- epic card (not when inside the epic view). A collapsible tree
        -- overlay lists only epics that are relevant reparent targets: it
        -- excludes the target epic and its descendants (cycle prevention at
        -- the UI layer), epics in Done or Archived status, and epics that are
        -- hidden by the active board filter (repo include/exclude and the
        -- only-active filter). The board search query is deliberately NOT
        -- applied here — this picker has its own input buffer, so inheriting
        -- the board query would hide otherwise-valid targets. See
        -- board_search_filter in board-layout.allium. When status filtering removes a
        -- parent but keeps
        -- an eligible child, the child is re-rooted to the top level so it
        -- stays selectable. The first item is always "— no parent —", which
        -- sets parent_epic_id to null.
        -- Tree navigation: j/k = up/down, l/Space/→ = expand,
        -- h/← = collapse, Enter = select.
        -- A [y/n] confirmation prompt is shown before committing the
        -- change. Pressing n returns to the picker; Esc/q cancels entirely.
        -- The confirmation is UI sugar — the normative outcome is the
        -- reparent itself, after the user has confirmed.
        --
        -- Cycle guards mirror UpdateEpicViaMcp: the chosen parent must not
        -- have the target epic anywhere in its ancestor chain, and the epic
        -- cannot be its own parent. The UI tree already excludes ineligible
        -- targets, so these guards are a defensive backstop.
        --
        -- The repo-group guard is NOT a UI-layer concern: this flow patches
        -- the epic through the same service path as UpdateEpicViaMcp, so an
        -- attempt to move an origin = repo_group sub-epic fails there and the
        -- TUI reports the error. The picker does not pre-filter on origin, so
        -- a repo-group sub-epic can be selected and only then rejected.
        -- updated_at is refreshed automatically by the database layer on
        -- any patch_epic call; the assertion above documents the intent.
}

rule ToggleAutoDispatch {
    when: UserTogglesAutoDispatch(epic)

    ensures:
        epic.auto_dispatch = not epic.auto_dispatch

    @guidance
        -- Triggered by pressing U in the epic detail view (i.e. while
        -- ViewMode::Epic is active). The key is a no-op outside that
        -- view.
        -- Flips the auto_dispatch flag. When false, closing a subtask's
        -- agent session no longer chains: AutoDispatchNextSubtask leaves
        -- the epic untouched and the next subtask is dispatched by the
        -- human from the TUI.
        -- The new value is persisted to the database and reflected
        -- immediately in the header indicator (green = auto, muted = manual).
}

-- == Automatic Subtask Chaining ==

rule AutoDispatchNextSubtask {
    when: SessionClosed(task)

    -- Deliberately no requires. A chain that stops is a normal outcome, not a
    -- violated precondition, so every guard lives inside the ensures below:
    -- no epic, auto_dispatch = false, or no backlog subtask each leave the
    -- epic untouched rather than rejecting the trigger.
    let epic = task.epic

    ensures:
        if epic != null and epic.auto_dispatch:
            -- First backlog subtask, ordered by sort_order (nulls fall back to
            -- the task id), then by id. Phoenix subtasks are not candidates —
            -- see PhoenixIsNeverChained below.
            let next = first_by_order(epic.subtasks where status = backlog and not phoenix)
            if next != null:
                DispatchTask(next)

    @guidance
        -- Server-side epic chaining. SessionClosed is emitted last by the MCP
        -- session-close flow (see ExitSessionViaMcp in mcp-task-tools.allium
        -- and ExitSession in pr-workflow.allium), so the next subtask's
        -- worktree is always cut from a base_branch that already contains the
        -- finished subtask's work.
        --
        -- exit_session is the ONLY emitter of SessionClosed, and that is the
        -- whole point. Reaching it requires a wrap_up first, and wrap_up is
        -- where the work is made visible to the next subtask: action = rebase
        -- rebases onto base_branch server-side, action = pr records the PR the
        -- work landed in, action = done is the caller stating the task needed
        -- neither. All three chain. What does NOT chain is any other route
        -- into Done — update_task(status = "done") (MarkTaskDoneViaMcp in
        -- mcp-task-tools.allium) and moving a card to Done in the TUI both
        -- close the task without emitting SessionClosed, because neither has a
        -- wrap_up behind it and so neither can say anything about base_branch.
        -- Chaining off those would launch a successor against an arbitrary
        -- base on the say-so of a caller who was never in the closing task's
        -- session.
        --
        -- SessionClosed does not follow every attempted close, only the ones
        -- that took effect. The close is a single write carrying the terminal
        -- status and the cleared tmux_window; when that write fails, the
        -- session-close rules deliberately withhold SessionClosed and this rule
        -- never runs. That is intentional, not a gap: the closing subtask is
        -- still sitting in its old status with its work unrecorded, and adding a
        -- freshly launched successor on top of that is strictly harder for a
        -- human to notice than the failed close on its own — the successor would
        -- also branch from a base whose fast-forward state the failed close says
        -- nothing about. The epic simply waits, exactly as it does for the
        -- normal stopping conditions below, and the human closes the subtask by
        -- hand and dispatches the next one from the TUI.
        --
        -- There is no agent-facing tool for this. The former dispatch_next MCP
        -- tool is gone precisely because, alongside automatic chaining, an
        -- agent that called it and then exited fired TWO subtasks — one of them
        -- from a base that predated its own commits, which is the bug this rule
        -- exists to remove.
        --
        -- Ordering guarantee. Every database write that determines the next
        -- subtask's starting base is complete before selection happens: the
        -- rebase / fast-forward of base_branch (WrapUpRebase) and the closing
        -- task's terminal status patch both precede this rule. The one thing
        -- still in flight is the closing task's tmux window teardown, which is
        -- detached and may overlap the new window's creation; window names are
        -- keyed by task id, so the closing and opening windows cannot collide.
        -- The guarantee is about database state, not OS-level teardown.
        --
        -- Five ways the chain stops, all normal outcomes: the task was closed
        -- by a route other than exit_session (see above); the closed task has
        -- no epic; the epic has auto_dispatch = false (see ToggleAutoDispatch);
        -- the epic has no backlog subtask left; or every backlog subtask it has
        -- left is a phoenix one (see PhoenixIsNeverChained). In each case the epic is
        -- untouched and the close reports a plain "session closed" response,
        -- and the human dispatches the next subtask from the TUI — the same
        -- recovery path auto_dispatch = false already produces. (A close whose
        -- terminal write failed also leaves the epic untouched, but that is a
        -- failure rather than a normal outcome and its response text says so;
        -- see above.)
        --
        -- PhoenixIsNeverChained. A subtask with phoenix = true is never a
        -- candidate, in any epic, whatever its position. Two reasons, and the
        -- first is a hard one:
        --
        --   1. It closes a loop that would otherwise have no floor. A phoenix
        --      subtask that finished would respawn into backlog (tasks.allium:
        --      PhoenixRespawn) and this rule would immediately dispatch the
        --      successor, which would finish, respawn, and be dispatched again
        --      — an epic that never runs out of work and never stops launching
        --      agents. The guard lives HERE rather than in PhoenixRespawn
        --      because the respawn is not the questionable half: creating a
        --      backlog task is always safe, and launching an agent at it
        --      unattended is the step that needs the veto.
        --   2. It is what a phoenix task means. The flag exists so a recurring
        --      task can be queued without being scheduled — the human picks the
        --      moment (see the head of the Phoenix section in tasks.allium).
        --      A phoenix task that auto-dispatched would be a scheduled task
        --      with extra steps.
        --
        -- The skip is a filter, not a stop: the chain passes OVER a phoenix
        -- subtask and dispatches the next ordinary backlog subtask behind it.
        -- An epic whose only remaining backlog subtasks are phoenix ones stops,
        -- and that is the fourth normal stopping condition alongside the three
        -- below.
        --
        -- It belongs to the CHAIN and to nothing else. Human dispatch is
        -- deliberately unaffected: pressing Space on a phoenix backlog card
        -- claims and launches it exactly as it would any other, which is the
        -- entire point of the flag (see the head of the Phoenix section in
        -- tasks.allium — the recurrence is in the queueing, not the running).
        -- Concretely, the two claim statements differ here and must keep
        -- differing: the by-epic claim excludes phoenix rows, the by-id claim
        -- (DispatchClaimExclusive, dispatch.allium) does not. Adding the filter
        -- to both would make a phoenix task undispatchable.
        --
        -- Candidate selection. Only direct Task children are candidates,
        -- ordered by sort_order ascending with the task id both as the fallback
        -- for nulls and as the tiebreaker. Sub-epics are NEVER auto-dispatched:
        -- if an epic's remaining work is nested inside a sub-epic, the human
        -- operator must dispatch that sub-epic explicitly from the TUI.
        -- Follow-up tasks created between wrap_up and the close (e.g. by the
        -- /retro skill) carry no sort_order, so their fallback key is their own
        -- — always larger — id: they sort after every planned subtask and are
        -- only ever chosen once the planned subtasks are exhausted.
        --
        -- At most one agent is launched per closed session, even under
        -- concurrent closes. Selection is exclusive because selecting and
        -- claiming are the SAME write: one conditional statement whose ordering
        -- predicate picks the first backlog subtask and whose update applies to
        -- exactly that row (implementation: UPDATE tasks ... WHERE id =
        -- (SELECT ... WHERE status = 'backlog' ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1) RETURNING
        -- id, serialised through the single writer connection). There is no
        -- select-then-claim window to lose, so there is no re-selection and no
        -- retry budget: the statement either returns the subtask it just
        -- claimed, or returns nothing because none was left. Two sessions
        -- closing on the same epic in the same instant therefore launch two
        -- DIFFERENT subtasks, never two agents for one subtask. The claim is a
        -- mechanism rather than domain behaviour, so it is recorded here instead
        -- of being modelled as a rule — but the exclusivity it buys is
        -- normative. The three stopping conditions above are consequently
        -- exhaustive: contention is not a fourth way for the chain to stop.
        -- Scope of that exclusivity: it is unconditional. Every dispatch entry
        -- point claims before it provisions — this chain, DispatchTaskViaMcp and
        -- the TUI dispatch key alike (DispatchClaimExclusive in
        -- dispatch.allium) — so no two of them can provision the same subtask.
        -- The other two claim a row they were handed rather than selecting one,
        -- but it is literally the same statement with the ordering subquery
        -- replaced by an id: one conditional write, same fields, backlog ->
        -- running only while the row is still backlog. This chain therefore
        -- cannot be beaten to a subtask it already holds, and a subtask another
        -- entry point claimed first is simply no longer backlog, so it is not a
        -- candidate here — which is stopping condition three, not a fourth.
        -- Consequence, on every path and not only this one: the status half of
        -- DispatchTask's postcondition (backlog -> running with the default
        -- running sub_status, and a seeded last_pre_tool_use_at) is applied
        -- synchronously at claim time, ahead of worktree provisioning, so a
        -- task being dispatched is briefly running with worktree = null. Both
        -- crash detection and activity classification filter on tmux_window !=
        -- null, so that window produces no spurious Stale or Crashed (see
        -- agent-health.allium), and is_wrappable requires a worktree, so the
        -- subtask cannot be wrapped up before it is provisioned.
        --
        -- The dispatch itself runs on a background task through the standard
        -- single-dispatch path, and a refresh notification is sent to the TUI
        -- when it completes. The ensures above emits DispatchTask, matching
        -- every other launch site in the specs (DispatchTaskViaMcp,
        -- JumpToAgentWindow); routing by tag is owned by dispatch.allium, so a
        -- plan-less research-tagged subtask reaches DispatchResearchTask
        -- instead. If the dispatch fails, the subtask is
        -- reverted to backlog with the default backlog sub_status, the
        -- last_pre_tool_use_at the claim seeded is cleared again, and
        -- AutoDispatchFailed is emitted (see SurfaceAutoDispatchFailure),
        -- leaving it dispatchable exactly as before the chain fired.
        -- That release is itself conditional, mirroring the claim: it applies
        -- only while the subtask is still running with no worktree, so a task
        -- that was provisioned or moved on during the dispatch attempt is left
        -- alone rather than stomped back to backlog. A release that does not
        -- apply is logged and otherwise ignored.
        -- The last_pre_tool_use_at clear is the one sanctioned exception to the
        -- ownership invariant in agent-health.allium: it unwinds a stamp this
        -- same flow wrote moments earlier, rather than overwriting a hook's.
        --
        -- No chain failure can fail the close. By the time this rule runs the
        -- closing task has already reached its terminal status and its session
        -- has been torn down, so reporting an error here would tell the agent
        -- its session failed to close when it did not. So the failure travels
        -- to the OPERATOR instead of to the closing agent: a failed dispatch
        -- emits AutoDispatchFailed, which SurfaceAutoDispatchFailure turns into
        -- board-visible evidence. A missing epic and a claim error stay
        -- log-only, because both fail before a subtask has been selected: there
        -- is no card to mark and no id to name, and both are indistinguishable
        -- from the normal stops above. A DB error while READING auto_dispatch is fail-CLOSED —
        -- the chain simply stops, indistinguishable from the normal stops above.
        -- Nothing requested this chain, so the conservative default is to launch
        -- no agent: failing open would start an agent on an epic whose operator
        -- may have turned chaining off. (The predecessor dispatch_next tool
        -- failed open, which was right for an explicit agent request; that
        -- reasoning does not transfer to an automatic trigger.)
}

rule SurfaceAutoDispatchFailure {
    when: AutoDispatchFailed(task, epic, reason)

    ensures: ChainStallMarked(task: task, epic: epic, reason: reason)
    ensures:
        if notifications_enabled:
            DesktopNotification(
                task: task,
                urgency: urgent,
                about: auto_dispatch_failed
            )

    @guidance
        -- AutoDispatchFailed is emitted by AutoDispatchNextSubtask when the
        -- chained dispatch it started does not provision — the provisioning
        -- itself failed, or the worker died attempting it. Both arms have
        -- already released the claim by the time this fires, so the subtask is
        -- back in backlog and the epic has stopped progressing. Without this
        -- rule the only evidence is a warn line in the application log, which
        -- no operator is watching: an epic that stalls on a transient network
        -- failure looks identical to one that has run out of backlog subtasks.
        --
        -- Three surfaces, deliberately, because they fail at different
        -- distances from the board. The status message (see
        -- AutoDispatchFailureIndicator) reaches an operator who is looking at
        -- it; the desktop notification reaches one who is not, and is gated on
        -- notifications_enabled exactly as NotifyNeedsInput and NotifyReview
        -- are (agent-health.allium); ChainStallMarked is what remains once both
        -- have passed, and is the only one that is still there an hour later.
        -- Urgency is urgent rather than normal: unlike a review becoming
        -- available, nothing further happens on this epic until a human acts.
        --
        -- Nothing here reports back to the closing agent. Its session is
        -- already closed and its close already succeeded; see the "No chain
        -- failure can fail the close" paragraph in AutoDispatchNextSubtask.
        --
        -- The two log-only stops (unresolvable epic, claim error) do not reach
        -- this rule, and that is a scope decision rather than an omission: both
        -- fail before a subtask is selected, so neither can supply the task
        -- this rule marks.
}

-- == Feed Task Identity Invariant ==
--
-- Within any role sub-epic's subtree (the role sub-epic itself plus its
-- direct repo-group children), no two feed tasks may share the same
-- external_id. This is the global identity guarantee that allows
-- run_role_routed_feed_sync to move tasks across role sub-epics and
-- across group_by_repo transitions without creating duplicates.
--
-- Enforced at the database level by triggers added in migration v72:
-- enforce_feed_task_subtree_unique_insert (BEFORE INSERT)
-- enforce_feed_task_subtree_unique_update (BEFORE UPDATE OF epic_id)
--
-- The feed respects this invariant by building its existing-task index
-- across the full subtree (repo-group children first, role sub-epic last
-- so role sub-epic copies take precedence) and always running subtree
-- stale deletion for every role sub-epic regardless of group_by_repo.

rule ToggleGroupByRepo {
    when: UserTogglesGroupByRepo(epic)

    ensures:
        epic.group_by_repo = not epic.group_by_repo
    ensures:
        if epic.feed_command = null:
            -- group_by_repo is already flipped at this point, so
            -- true ⇒ just turned ON ⇒ Regroup; false ⇒ just turned OFF ⇒ Flatten.
            if epic.group_by_repo:
                RegroupEpic(epic)
            else:
                FlattenEpic(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Triggered by pressing R in the epic detail view (ViewMode::Epic).
        -- A no-op outside that view.
        -- The flag flip is unconditional: the new value is persisted
        -- immediately for any epic (feed or non-feed) and reflected in the
        -- header indicator (green "group:on [R]" when true, muted
        -- "group:off [R]" when false).
        --
        -- EXCEPT when the write is REFUSED. The keypress flips the board's own
        -- copy of the flag first and asks the write path second, so the header
        -- redraws before the outcome is known. UpdateEpicViaMcp's requires
        -- refuses grouping on an append-only epic, and that is a refusal a
        -- user can reach by simply pressing R — the key is offered on every
        -- epic. On refusal the header is restored from the persisted epic, not
        -- merely accompanied by an error popup: otherwise the row would go on
        -- claiming "append-only  group:on [R]", a pair the system does not
        -- allow, until the next timed refresh happened to correct it. See
        -- AppendOnlyEpicIndicator.
        -- The task migration (RegroupEpic / FlattenEpic) only runs for
        -- non-feed epics (feed_command = null):
        --   * turning ON  → RegroupEpic moves every non-archived task into
        --     its per-repo sub-epic (creating sub-epics as needed);
        --   * turning OFF → FlattenEpic pulls every task from repo-group
        --     sub-epics back to the parent.
        -- For feed epics the flag still flips but no migration fires
        -- SYNCHRONOUSLY from the keypress: repo-grouping is driven by the feed
        -- ingestion itself, so triggering a migration directly from the toggle
        -- would duplicate that code path. The non-feed guard lives HERE, in
        -- this toggle handler, and nowhere else: it re-reads the epic after the
        -- flag write and only calls RegroupEpic/FlattenEpic when
        -- feed_command = null. The two migration rules do not re-check it
        -- themselves (see their own guidance), so a feed epic is spared a
        -- migration because this caller declines to ask for one, not because
        -- the migration would refuse.
        -- Instead, BOTH directions are reconciled on the NEXT feed sync (auto
        -- poll or manual "r"), not synchronously on toggle:
        --   * turning ON  → GroupedFeedUpsert migrates pre-existing flat tasks
        --     into per-repo sub-epics (feeds.allium: "doubles as migration");
        --   * turning OFF → FlatFeedReconcile re-homes tasks from the epic's
        --     RepoGroup sub-epics back to the parent and deletes the now-empty
        --     sub-epics (feeds.allium), the OFF-side mirror of that migration.
        -- The toggle handler itself stays a pure flag flip for feed epics; the
        -- self-healing is poll-driven and symmetric in both directions.
}

rule RegroupEpic {
    when: RegroupEpic(epic)

    requires: epic.group_by_repo
    requires: epic.feed_command = null

    ensures:
        for task in epic.subtasks where task.status != archived:
            task.epic = route_target(epic, task.repo_path)
            RecalculateEpicStatus(task.epic)
    ensures: RecalculateEpicStatus(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Each destination sub-epic is recalculated as tasks land in it (not
        -- only the grouped root): a sub-epic that just received tasks must
        -- leave done/archived if it had settled there empty.
        --
        -- Both preconditions above are enforced by the TUI caller
        -- (ToggleGroupByRepo, which flips the flag and then picks
        -- RegroupEpic vs FlattenEpic by its new value, and only for a
        -- non-feed epic), not by the handler itself: the migration is an
        -- unconditional walk over the epic's tasks. Same precedent as
        -- DeleteTask's archived-status precondition (tasks.allium).
        -- There is no second caller today; a new one would have to
        -- re-establish both guards for itself.
        --
        -- route_target(epic, repo_path) returns the existing per-repo
        -- sub-epic for that repo path, creating one (with origin =
        -- repo_group) if none exists yet. The sub-epic title is derived
        -- from the repo path's base name.
        -- Only non-archived tasks are moved; archived tasks stay where
        -- they are.
        -- A grouped epic should have no direct tasks after this runs:
        -- every non-archived task is routed into a sub-epic.
        -- RegroupEpic is idempotent: calling it when tasks are already
        -- in the correct sub-epic is a no-op for each task.
        -- Dispatching a grouped epic operates per sub-epic, not on the
        -- parent directly: only direct Task children are dispatch
        -- candidates (see AutoDispatchNextSubtask), so the human selects a
        -- sub-epic to dispatch. Sub-epics are one level deep — sub-epics
        -- of repo-group sub-epics are not created.
        --
        -- Progress aggregation agrees across surfaces: the TUI's
        -- epic-status indicator (●N counts by status column) aggregates
        -- across all active_children (tasks + sub-epics) recursively, and
        -- the MCP tools (GetEpicViaMcp, ListEpicsViaMcp) share one rollup
        -- that likewise aggregates every descendant sub-epic's tasks for a
        -- group_by_repo epic (direct subtasks only for any other epic). A
        -- grouped root whose tasks all live in per-repo sub-epics therefore
        -- reports the real totals to agents and to the board alike.
}

rule FlattenEpic {
    when: FlattenEpic(epic)

    requires: epic.group_by_repo = false
    requires: epic.feed_command = null

    ensures:
        for sub in epic.sub_epics where sub.origin = repo_group and sub.status != archived:
            for task in sub.subtasks: task.epic = epic
            -- (sub.subtasks.count evaluated after the task-move above, i.e. post-state)
            -- reduces to: sub had no nested sub-epics and all its tasks were just moved out.
            if sub.subtasks.count = 0 and sub.sub_epics.count = 0:
                DeleteEpic(sub)
            else:
                RecalculateEpicStatus(sub)
    ensures: RecalculateEpicStatus(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Every one of the sub-epic's own direct tasks is moved to the root
        -- before the emptiness check runs, so the only way it is left
        -- non-empty (DeleteEpic skipped) is nested sub-epics of its own —
        -- flatten_epic re-homes only direct tasks, one level. A sub-epic left
        -- non-empty this way IS still recalculated (the same shared cleanup
        -- helper recalculates whichever branch it takes: delete, or
        -- recalculate-and-keep), so its status reflects the tasks it just
        -- lost even though it survives.
        --
        -- As with RegroupEpic, both preconditions above are enforced by the
        -- TUI caller (ToggleGroupByRepo), not by the handler itself: the
        -- re-home walk runs unconditionally on whatever epic it is handed.
        --
        -- Moves all tasks from repo-group sub-epics back to the parent
        -- epic. Only non-archived sub-epics (origin = repo_group) are
        -- affected; sub-epics the user created manually are left alone.
        -- A repo-group sub-epic that ends up with no tasks and no
        -- sub-epics is deleted (hard delete, not archived).
        -- FlattenEpic is idempotent: calling it when no repo-group
        -- sub-epics exist is a no-op.
        -- After flattening, RecalculateEpicStatus is called to update
        -- the parent's status based on its new direct children.
}

-- == Managed Feed Provisioning ==

rule ProvisionManagedEpics {
    when: ProvisionManagedEpics()

    -- Idempotently materialises the managed feed epic tree from the
    -- config surface above. Fires on TUI startup, reading the current
    -- reviews/CVE feed configuration, AND whenever the config is saved via
    -- SetManagedFeedConfigViaMcp. Running it repeatedly converges on the same
    -- tree without creating duplicates: every managed epic is matched by the PAIR
    -- (feed_role, expected parent) — NEVER by title, so a user who renames
    -- any managed epic keeps that rename across re-runs. The parent half of
    -- that key means recognition is position-sensitive; see the reparenting
    -- caveat in the guidance below.
    --
    -- Two independent subtrees, each provisioned ONLY when its command
    -- is configured (opt-in):
    --
    --   reviews subtree (when reviews_feed_command != null):
    --     - a reviews_parent ROOT epic (parent = null) carrying the
    --       reviews feed_command + feed_interval_secs;
    --     - three role SUB-epics under it — my_reviews, team_reviews,
    --       bots — each with feed_command = null.
    --
    --   cve epic (when cve_feed_command != null):
    --     - a cve ROOT epic (parent = null) carrying the CVE
    --       feed_command + feed_interval_secs.
    --
    -- Per-role ensure policy: if an ACTIVE (non-archived) epic with that
    -- role already exists, keep it and leave its title untouched; for the
    -- command-carrying roles only, update feed_command/feed_interval_secs
    -- when the configured value differs. If an ARCHIVED epic with that role
    -- exists, leave it archived (do NOT resurrect), warn, and create
    -- nothing. Otherwise create the epic and stamp its feed_role.
    --
    -- active_epic_for_role / archived_epic_for_role are black-box lookups
    -- SCOPED TO THE EXPECTED PARENT for that role (null for the two root
    -- roles, the reviews_parent root for the three review sub-roles): each
    -- returns the single Epic with the given feed_role AND that parent, in
    -- the given state, or null when none exists. Singularity is guaranteed
    -- by core/Epic.UniqueFeedRolePerParent (at most one epic per
    -- (parent, feed_role)). An epic carrying the role under some OTHER
    -- parent does not satisfy the lookup.

    ensures:
        -- Reviews subtree — opt-in on reviews_feed_command.
        if reviews_feed_command != null:
            -- reviews_parent ROOT (command-carrying).
            let active_parent = active_epic_for_role(reviews_parent)
            if exists active_parent:
                -- Keep title; reconcile only the command/interval.
                if active_parent.feed_command != reviews_feed_command:
                    active_parent.feed_command = reviews_feed_command
                if active_parent.feed_interval_secs != reviews_feed_interval_secs:
                    active_parent.feed_interval_secs = reviews_feed_interval_secs
            else if exists archived_epic_for_role(reviews_parent):
                log_warn("managed epic for role reviews_parent is archived; not resurrecting")
            else:
                core/Epic.created(
                    title:              "PR Reviews",
                    status:             backlog,
                    parent:             null,
                    feed_role:          reviews_parent,
                    feed_command:       reviews_feed_command,
                    feed_interval_secs: reviews_feed_interval_secs,
                    created_at:         now,
                    updated_at:         now
                )

            -- Three role SUB-epics, all feed_command = null. They are
            -- reconciled only via the parent's role router; the FeedRunner
            -- never polls them independently. Their parent is the (now
            -- guaranteed to exist if not archived) reviews_parent root.
            let parent_root = active_epic_for_role(reviews_parent)
            if exists parent_root:
                for role in {my_reviews, team_reviews, bots}:
                    if not exists active_epic_for_role(role):
                        if exists archived_epic_for_role(role):
                            log_warn("managed sub-epic for role is archived; not resurrecting")
                        else:
                            core/Epic.created(
                                title:        managed_role_title(role),
                                status:       backlog,
                                parent:       parent_root,
                                feed_role:    role,
                                feed_command: null,
                                created_at:   now,
                                updated_at:   now
                            )

        -- CVE epic — opt-in on cve_feed_command.
        if cve_feed_command != null:
            let active_cve = active_epic_for_role(cve)
            if exists active_cve:
                if active_cve.feed_command != cve_feed_command:
                    active_cve.feed_command = cve_feed_command
                if active_cve.feed_interval_secs != cve_feed_interval_secs:
                    active_cve.feed_interval_secs = cve_feed_interval_secs
            else if exists archived_epic_for_role(cve):
                log_warn("managed epic for role cve is archived; not resurrecting")
            else:
                core/Epic.created(
                    title:              "CVE",
                    status:             backlog,
                    parent:             null,
                    feed_role:          cve,
                    feed_command:       cve_feed_command,
                    feed_interval_secs: cve_feed_interval_secs,
                    created_at:         now,
                    updated_at:         now
                )

    @guidance
        -- No feed_command on sub-epics — the B3 concurrency guard.
        -- The my_reviews / team_reviews / bots sub-epics deliberately
        -- carry feed_command = null. Because FeedTick (feeds.allium)
        -- only polls epics where feed_command != null, the sub-epics
        -- are NEVER polled on their own. Their tasks are produced solely
        -- by the reviews_parent root's single emission, split across the
        -- buckets by the RouteSignals router (feeds.allium). Giving a
        -- sub-epic its own command would let two pollers write the same
        -- bucket concurrently — the race this guard exists to prevent.
        --
        -- Rename-stable, archived-not-resurrected. Managed epics are
        -- matched by (feed_role, parent), never by title, so a user rename of any
        -- managed epic (root or sub) survives every re-run — only the
        -- command-carrying roles (reviews_parent, cve) ever have their
        -- feed_command/feed_interval_secs reconciled, and titles are
        -- never written for an existing epic. An archived managed epic
        -- is left archived: provisioning logs a warning and creates no
        -- duplicate. Re-enabling a managed feed after archiving is an
        -- explicit user action (unarchive from the TUI), not something
        -- this rule does automatically.
        --
        -- Opt-in. Each subtree is provisioned only when its command is
        -- configured. A null reviews_feed_command means no reviews_parent
        -- and no sub-epics; a null cve_feed_command means no cve epic.
        -- Clearing a command does NOT delete an already-provisioned
        -- subtree — teardown is a separate, explicit user action.
        --
        -- Idempotency & race-safety, and its limit. Re-running creates no
        -- duplicates AS LONG AS the managed epics still sit where
        -- provisioning expects them: each role is looked up as
        -- (feed_role, expected parent), and the
        -- core/Epic.UniqueFeedRolePerParent invariant (a partial unique
        -- index on (parent, feed_role) WHERE feed_role != none, keyed as
        -- COALESCE(parent, -1) so root-level roles collide too — see that
        -- invariant in core.allium for why) makes a duplicate AT THAT SAME
        -- PARENT physically impossible. Two instances starting up
        -- concurrently therefore cannot both insert the same role sub-epic:
        -- `EpicCrud::create_managed_role_epic` inserts feed_role in the same
        -- statement as the row itself, and the second insert loses to the
        -- unique index and re-selects the winner rather than creating a
        -- duplicate. The one exception is a database that already held
        -- duplicate managed epics before the index was fixed to be
        -- NULL-safe — that index was never built for it, so its
        -- provisioning stays unprotected until the duplicates are resolved
        -- manually.
        --
        -- Reparenting caveat (known consequence of the parent half of the
        -- key). Idempotency is NOT position-independent. If a user reparents
        -- a managed epic — moving a role sub-epic out from under the
        -- reviews_parent root, or making the reviews_parent root itself a
        -- child of something — the next provisioning run does not recognise
        -- it, because its parent no longer matches the expected one. The run
        -- then creates a FRESH epic for that role, which the unique index
        -- permits (the parent differs), leaving two epics carrying the same
        -- feed_role. The moved epic keeps its tasks; the new one starts
        -- empty. Recovering means reparenting the original back into place
        -- and deleting the duplicate by hand. Matching on feed_role alone
        -- would remove this hazard but would also make the (parent, role)
        -- index non-authoritative; the trade-off is unresolved (see the open
        -- question below).
        --
        -- managed_role_title(role) supplies the default display title for
        -- a freshly-created sub-epic (e.g. my_reviews -> "My Reviews",
        -- team_reviews -> "Team Reviews", bots -> "Bots"). It is consulted
        -- only on creation; once an epic exists its title is owned by the
        -- user.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- EpicStatusRecalculation:
-- Epic status is persisted and recalculated via recalculate_epic_status.
    --
    -- WHERE IT IS DERIVED. Wherever the rows live, and by whatever holds all of
    -- them. On a single-machine install that is the local store and this has
    -- never been an interesting question. On a board sharing a store it is the
    -- STORE, and it has to be.
    --
    -- The reason is that no board sees all of an epic's children. An epic's
    -- subtasks can be owned by different people on different machines, and a
    -- board receives only what it subscribes to (sync.allium:
    -- SubscriptionsCoverOnlyTheOwnBoardAndItsEpics). A board deriving the
    -- status from what it can see is deriving it from a subset, so:
    --
    --   * a board that sees only the done children concludes "all children
    --     done" and writes done;
    --   * a board that sees a running one writes the regression back to
    --     backlog;
    --   * both are following this derivation correctly, and the epic flips
    --     between the two answers for as long as both boards are up.
    --
    -- That flapping is not a race to be tightened away with locking. It is two
    -- correct answers to two different questions, and the only fix is to stop
    -- asking the partial one. So the derivation runs once, at the store, over
    -- every child — and a board never computes it, not even for its own epics,
    -- because "its own" is not a property the epic has.
    --
    -- What a board still does is TRIGGER it: the trigger points below are
    -- mutations, the mutations go to the store, and the store recalculates as
    -- part of the same all-or-nothing change (sync.allium:
    -- EveryMutationIsAtomicAndAnswered). The new status therefore arrives at
    -- every subscribed board the same way any other row does, rather than being
    -- computed again on each of them.
    -- It moves both forward and backward to always reflect child reality.
    -- "Children" means active_children: the union of active_subtasks (Task)
    -- and active_sub_epics (Epic). Each child contributes its .status value.
    --
    -- Derivation logic:
    --   epic is archived => no change (highest-priority case: archived is
    --     terminal for this recalculation, so an archived epic never flips
    --     back to done just because a newly-attached child happens to be
    --     all-done — ArchiveEpic archives a whole subtree and then
    --     recalculates ancestors)
    --   all active children done (≥1 active child) => done
    --   epic is done AND has active non-done children => backlog  (regression)
    --   otherwise => no change (epic stays in current status)
    --
    --   The "no active children" case does not auto-set to backlog — the epic
    --   keeps its current status. A freshly-created epic starts as backlog via
    --   the CreateEpic rule, so no information is lost.
    --   A child whose status this binary does not recognise stops the
    --   derivation: nothing is written, and the epic keeps what it has. Both
    --   guesses are wrong in a way nobody can see — treating it as done
    --   finishes an epic that is not finished, and treating it as unfinished
    --   holds one open forever with nothing saying why. Declining leaves the
    --   next recalculation, perhaps by a newer binary, free to get it right.
    --   The realistic producer is a colleague on a newer version, which only
    --   exists once the children can come from more than one machine.
    --   The running and review statuses are never set automatically; they are
    --   only reachable via manual MoveEpicForward / MoveEpicBackward actions.
    --   Manual placements (including running / review) are preserved across
    --   subsequent recalc calls — only the two auto-transitions above will
    --   override a manual placement.
    --
    -- The new status is written only when one of the two auto-transitions
    -- above fires; otherwise the DB row is left untouched.
    --
    -- The all-active-children-done => done auto-transition also stamps
    -- epic.completed_at = now, using the identical rule as tasks (see
    -- tasks.allium's ConfirmDone). The done-with-active-non-done-children =>
    -- backlog regression does NOT clear it: completed_at records the last
    -- completion, and a regression does not unmake one — the next
    -- all-children-done transition overwrites it. A no-op recalculation
    -- (status does not change) never touches it, mirroring the "DB row is left
    -- untouched" rule above. epic.sort_order is untouched by either
    -- transition: it is manual ordering and nothing else.
    --
    -- This is also the one write that can discard a manual Done-column reorder
    -- of the epic's card, which persists an override into the same field
    -- (tasks.allium, ReorderItem). Accepted: the epic genuinely finished at
    -- this moment, and a real completion outranks an earlier hand-placement.
    --
    -- Trigger points: recalculate_epic_status must be called whenever the
    -- set of active children or their statuses change. This includes:
    --   * task status transitions (patch_task, wrap_up, dispatch, etc.)
    --   * task creation or deletion within the epic
    --   * feed task upserts (upsert_feed_tasks) — both flat and grouped;
    --     for grouped feeds, each sub-epic's recalculation propagates upward
    --     to the parent automatically via the Propagation rule below.
    --   * sub-epic creation or status change
    --
    -- Propagation: after recalculating an epic's status, if the epic has a
    -- parent, recalculate_epic_status is called on the parent as well.
    -- This propagates status changes up the full ancestry chain, to a bounded
    -- depth. The bound is not a modelling choice — no real ancestry approaches
    -- it — it is what turns a corrupt parent chain into one refused
    -- recalculation rather than an unbounded walk that takes the whole store
    -- down with it.
    -- Implementation note: the propagation walk threads a `visited` set
    -- to defensively break parent_epic_id cycles, even though the
    -- `parent_epic_id != id` CHECK plus immutable parent_epic_id make
    -- cycles unreachable in practice.
    --
    -- Epic substatus (display label for epic cards):
    --   backlog + no plan             => unplanned
    --   backlog + has plan            => planned
    --   running + any child blocked   => "N blocked" (N = count of running
    --     tasks with sub_status in {needs_input, stale, crashed, conflict};
    --     running sub-epics do not directly contribute to blocked count)
    --   running + no blocked          => active
    --   review                        => in review
    --   done/archived                 => done
    --
    -- Epic cards show colored status indicators (●N) for each non-zero
    -- child count by parent status, using the column palette colors.
    -- Sub-epic children are counted alongside task children in the indicators.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface AutoDispatchFailureIndicator {
    facing user: User

    -- What ChainStallMarked (see SurfaceAutoDispatchFailure) looks like on the
    -- board: a subtask whose chained dispatch failed and was released back to
    -- backlog. Evaluated per rendered card, not only for the selected one.
    --
    -- The marked set is implementation-internal and is NOT persisted. It does
    -- not need to be: the chain runs inside the board's own process, so the
    -- marker is exactly as durable as the failure that produced it, and a
    -- board that has been restarted since is observing the epic afresh rather
    -- than replaying a stall it has already forgotten.

    context task: core/Task

    let chain_stalled = auto_dispatch_failed(task)

    exposes:
        chain_stalled

    @guarantee StatusMessageNamesTheSubtask
        -- The failure raises a status message naming the subtask, so an
        -- operator watching the board learns which chain stopped and why
        -- rather than only that something did. It is an ordinary transient
        -- message and expires on the usual status_message_expiry; the card
        -- indicator below is what carries the fact afterwards.

    @guarantee PersistsUntilRedispatched
        -- Unlike every other transient board signal, the card indicator does
        -- not decay on a timer. A stalled chain stays stalled until a human
        -- acts, so the evidence stays until a human acts: it is cleared when
        -- the subtask is dispatched again, when it leaves backlog by any other
        -- route (someone moved it, or another entry point claimed it), and
        -- when the task itself is removed from the board. Nothing else clears
        -- it. This is the whole point of the surface — a chain that died at
        -- 02:00 must still be visible at 09:00.

    @guarantee DispatchingOutranksIt
        -- While a dispatch is in flight for the subtask, the card shows the
        -- dispatching feedback (DispatchingFeedback in dispatch.allium), not
        -- this indicator. Retrying is the resolution, so a stale failure must
        -- never mask the retry's own spinner.

    @guarantee DistinctFromOrdinaryBacklog
        -- The indicator replaces the ordinary backlog age/plan indicator and
        -- is rendered in the same alarming style as the conflict, crashed and
        -- unprovisioned indicators. A released subtask is otherwise
        -- byte-identical to one that was never dispatched, which is precisely
        -- the confusion this removes.

    @guidance
        -- The epic header's auto-dispatch indicator (green = auto, muted =
        -- manual; see ToggleAutoDispatch) says only whether chaining is ON. It
        -- cannot say whether chaining is WORKING, and an operator reading a
        -- green indicator over a stalled epic is being actively misled. This
        -- surface is where "the chain tried and failed" lives.
}

surface AppendOnlyEpicIndicator {
    facing user: User

    -- What core/Epic.feed_append_only looks like on the epic header: the
    -- literal text `append-only`, in green, in the top indicator row.
    --
    -- That row already answers "what kind of epic is this" -- auto or manual
    -- dispatch (ToggleAutoDispatch), the feed routing role, repo grouping
    -- (ToggleGroupByRepo). Append-only is the remaining answer of that kind,
    -- and the one with the most visible consequence: such an epic's cards
    -- never clear, because absence from an emission removes nothing
    -- (feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed). Without a marker, a user looking at an
    -- epic whose cards only ever accumulate has nothing on screen explaining
    -- why.

    context epic: core/Epic

    -- Rendered only for an epic that has declared itself append-only.
    let visible = epic.feed_append_only

    exposes:
        visible

    @guarantee RenderedOnlyWhenTheFlagIsTrue
        -- The marker exists only for an epic whose flag is true; there is
        -- deliberately no `append:off` counterpart. This is the one place the
        -- row is asymmetric -- the grouping indicator always shows both
        -- states, "group:on [R]" and "group:off [R]" -- and the asymmetry is
        -- the point: nearly every epic is a mirroring one, so a false-state
        -- badge would be permanent clutter on the ordinary case in exchange
        -- for saying nothing about it.

    @guarantee NoKeyHintBecauseThereIsNoKey
        -- The marker carries no bracketed key, unlike "auto dispatch [U]" and
        -- "group:on [R]". In this row a bracket means "press this key to
        -- toggle", so its absence is what tells the user this is a property to
        -- read, not a control to press. That there is no keybinding for the
        -- flag is itself deliberate: turning it OFF gives the next feed cycle
        -- licence to remove every accumulated task the current emission does
        -- not name, which for a log feed is potentially all of them. The flag
        -- therefore belongs to wiring an epic up -- the MCP write path,
        -- UpdateEpicViaMcp -- rather than to a one-key board toggle. See
        -- feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed for the full argument.

    @guarantee SitsBetweenTheRoleAndGroupingSlots
        -- Position in the row: after the feed-role slot ("role:<role>", shown
        -- when feed_role != none) and before the grouping slot. The settled
        -- row is "manual dispatch [U]  append-only  group:off [R]", because
        -- UpdateEpicViaMcp refuses append-only together with grouping or a
        -- feed role and so neither neighbour's populated form is ever
        -- PERSISTED alongside the marker.
        --
        -- Never persisted is not the same as never rendered: pressing R on an
        -- append-only epic redraws the header before the refusal arrives. A
        -- marker whose job is to explain an epic must not be the thing that
        -- misleads about it, so that refusal restores the header rather than
        -- only reporting the error -- see ToggleGroupByRepo, which owns the
        -- mechanism and the argument for it.

    @guarantee ShownWhateverTheFeedCommand
        -- The flag is the only condition; there is no additional guard on
        -- feed_command. An epic carrying the flag with no feed command is a
        -- misconfiguration, and one the user is better off seeing than not --
        -- the marker is the only place that combination becomes visible.

    @guarantee EpicDetailViewOnly
        -- Like every other indicator in this row it renders in the epic detail
        -- view and never in board view (ViewMode::Board), which has no single
        -- epic to describe.
        --
        -- "Epic detail view" here means ViewMode::Epic, whatever the
        -- FLATTENED toggle is set to. Flattened is an orthogonal concept in
        -- this spec set — board-layout.allium's FlattenedView applies inside epic view
        -- too, where it widens the scope to the epic's whole subtree — and it
        -- does not change which epic the header describes. So a flattened
        -- epic view still shows the marker, and only leaving for board view
        -- takes it away.

    @guarantee ReadOnlyWithNoTrigger
        -- Purely a readout: rendering it changes no state, and there is no
        -- trigger this surface responds to. The flag is written only by
        -- UpdateEpicViaMcp and read only by the feed cycle
        -- (feeds.allium: AppendOnlyFeed) and by this row.

    @guidance
        -- Rendered by src/tui/ui/shared.rs::render_top_indicators, the same
        -- function that draws the dispatch-mode, feed-role and grouping slots,
        -- so all four share one line and its width handling rather than
        -- competing for space from separate call sites.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "R is offered on every epic in the detail view, including an append-only one where UpdateEpicViaMcp guarantees the toggle will be refused. AppendOnlyEpicIndicator argues that a bracketed key in the header means 'press this to toggle', so advertising [R] there contradicts the row's own convention -- yet the flag it would refuse is exactly the one the append-only marker exists to explain. Should the keypress be guarded before it is attempted (no refusal, no rollback, but the service rule is then duplicated in the TUI), should [R] render differently on such an epic, or is refusing-then-restoring the honest behaviour to keep?"

open question "Managed-epic role lookup keys on (feed_role, parent), so reparenting a managed epic makes the next ProvisionManagedEpics run create a duplicate epic for that role. Should the lookup fall back to feed_role alone, should managed epics be reparent-blocked the way repo-group sub-epics are, or is the duplicate an acceptable cost of a user moving them?"
